<div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Translating foreign transactions and operations under retained older terminology</div><div class="principle">ASPE Section 1651 governs HOW an ENTERPRISE translates FOREIGN currency TRANSACTIONS and FOREIGN operations INTO its OWN reporting CURRENCY. GENUINELY distinctively, ASPE RETAINS the PRE-2011 Canadian GAAP FRAMEWORK classifying FOREIGN operations as EITHER "INTEGRATED" or "SELF-SUSTAINING" — RATHER than ADOPTING IAS 21'S OWN "FUNCTIONAL currency" concept internationally (Section 5).</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Foreign currency transactions and foreign operations</div><div class="principle">This Section applies TO transactions DENOMINATED in a FOREIGN currency, and TO the TRANSLATION of FOREIGN operations' FINANCIAL statements FOR inclusion WITHIN the REPORTING enterprise'S own FINANCIAL statements.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Initial Recognition — Spot Rate at the Transaction Date</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Translating a foreign currency transaction on first recognition</div><div class="text-supporting">A FOREIGN currency TRANSACTION is INITIALLY recorded BY applying the SPOT exchange RATE at the DATE of the TRANSACTION — CONSISTENT with the GENERAL approach USED internationally UNDER IAS 21.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Monetary Items — Retranslated at Each Reporting Date</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Exchange differences generally recognized in net income</div><div class="text-supporting">FOREIGN currency MONETARY items (CASH, receivables, PAYABLES) are RETRANSLATED at the YEAR-END exchange RATE at EACH balance SHEET date, WITH the RESULTING exchange DIFFERENCES generally RECOGNIZED in NET income for the PERIOD.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>THE DISTINCTIVE FRAMEWORK — Integrated vs Self-Sustaining Foreign Operations</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Two categories, each with an entirely different translation method</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Category</span><span>Description</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Integrated foreign operation</span><span>A FOREIGN operation WHOSE activities ARE integral TO the REPORTING enterprise'S OWN operations — FINANCIALLY and OPERATIONALLY dependent ON the PARENT</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Self-sustaining foreign operation</span><span>A FOREIGN operation that IS financially AND operationally INDEPENDENT of the PARENT — GENERATING and USING cash PRIMARILY in ITS own LOCAL currency</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Integrated Foreign Operations — the Temporal Method</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Translation gains/losses flow directly through net income</div><div class="text-supporting">AN INTEGRATED foreign OPERATION'S financial STATEMENTS are TRANSLATED using the TEMPORAL method — MONETARY items AT the YEAR-end RATE, NON-monetary items AT their HISTORICAL rate, and REVENUE/expenses at AVERAGE or TRANSACTION-date rates — WITH the RESULTING translation GAINS and LOSSES recognized DIRECTLY within NET income, MUCH as IF the OPERATION were SIMPLY an EXTENSION of the PARENT'S own OPERATIONS.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Self-Sustaining Foreign Operations — the Current Rate Method</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Assets/liabilities at the closing rate, income/expenses at the average rate</div><div class="text-supporting">A SELF-SUSTAINING foreign OPERATION'S financial STATEMENTS are TRANSLATED using the CURRENT rate METHOD — ASSETS and LIABILITIES at the YEAR-end CLOSING rate, and INCOME and EXPENSES at the AVERAGE rate FOR the period — CONSISTENT with the GENERAL approach IAS 21 ALSO applies to FOREIGN operations translated USING their OWN distinct FUNCTIONAL currency.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Where the Self-Sustaining Translation Adjustment Goes — Bypassing Net Income</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Directly to a separate equity component, given ASPE's absence of OCI</div><div class="text-supporting">THE resulting TRANSLATION adjustment for a SELF-SUSTAINING foreign OPERATION is RECOGNIZED WITHIN a SEPARATE component OF equity — GIVEN ASPE'S complete ABSENCE of any OTHER comprehensive INCOME concept (see the COMPANION Section 1520 poster), this ADJUSTMENT CANNOT pass THROUGH an "OCI" layer the WAY it WOULD under IFRS. INSTEAD, it is RECOGNIZED DIRECTLY within a DEDICATED equity RESERVE, ENTIRELY bypassing NET income — a GENUINELY important STRUCTURAL consequence of ASPE'S broader ABSENCE of OCI, TYING directly BACK to the THEME running THROUGHOUT this ENTIRE poster series.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Reclassifying the Adjustment Upon Disposal</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Moving the accumulated adjustment into net income when the operation is sold</div><div class="text-supporting">UPON disposal (OR substantially COMPLETE liquidation) OF a SELF-SUSTAINING foreign OPERATION, the CUMULATIVE amount OF the translation ADJUSTMENT previously ACCUMULATED within the SEPARATE equity COMPONENT is RECLASSIFIED into NET income AS part of the GAIN or LOSS on DISPOSAL — a "RECYCLING"-style outcome, ACHIEVED directly FROM the equity RESERVE, RATHER than FROM an OCI layer as WOULD occur under IFRS.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Worked Example — Translating an Integrated Foreign Operation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Translation gains flowing directly through net income</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A US sales branch integral to the parent's operations</div><div class="wk-body">A Canadian company's US sales branch is financially and operationally dependent on its Canadian parent, qualifying as an integrated foreign operation. Applying the temporal method, the branch's monetary items generate a $45,000 translation gain during the year.</div><div class="wk-answer">The COMPANY recognizes the FULL $45,000 GAIN DIRECTLY within NET income FOR the period — CONSISTENT with the TEMPORAL method'S TREATMENT of INTEGRATED foreign OPERATIONS.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Worked Example — Translating a Self-Sustaining Foreign Operation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Bypassing net income entirely</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: An independently operating foreign subsidiary</div><div class="wk-body">The same Canadian company also owns a wholly independent German manufacturing subsidiary, financially and operationally self-sustaining, generating and using cash primarily in euros. Applying the current rate method, translating the subsidiary's net assets produces a $180,000 translation adjustment for the year.</div><div class="wk-answer">The COMPANY recognizes the $180,000 ADJUSTMENT DIRECTLY within a SEPARATE component OF equity — <strong>NOT</strong> within NET income — GIVEN ASPE'S complete ABSENCE of an OCI mechanism THROUGH which SUCH an ADJUSTMENT might OTHERWISE flow.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What Section 1651 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Amount of exchange gains and losses recognized in net income during the period</span><span>Classification of each significant foreign operation as integrated or self-sustaining</span><span>Reconciliation of the opening and closing balance of the separate equity component accumulating self-sustaining translation adjustments</span></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgment matters most</div>
<div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Classifying a foreign operation as integrated or self-sustaining, based on its genuine degree of financial and operational independence</span></div></div>
<div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Average exchange rate used as a practical approximation for translating income and expenses</span></div></div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Relationship to IAS 21's Functional Currency Approach</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — ASPE Section 1651 vs IAS 21</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">ASPE Section 1651</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IAS 21</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Classification framework</span><span>Integrated vs self-sustaining</span><span>Functional currency of each entity</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Self-sustaining/functional-currency translation adjustment</span><span>Separate equity component (no OCI)</span><span>Other comprehensive income, recycled on disposal</span></div>
</div>
<div class="note">CONCEPTUALLY analogous OUTCOMES (INTEGRATED ≈ SAME functional CURRENCY as the PARENT; self-SUSTAINING ≈ a DISTINCT functional CURRENCY), but ASPE RETAINS its OWN, older TERMINOLOGY and MECHANICALLY bypasses OCI entirely, GIVEN ASPE has NO such CONCEPT.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every foreign currency translation event lands</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Integrated operation translation</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge is">IS</span></span><span>Recognized directly in net income</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Self-sustaining operation translation</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge sre">SRE</span></span><span>Recognized within a separate equity component, bypassing net income entirely</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section</span><span>Connection</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section 1601</span><span>Consolidated Financial Statements — relies on this Section's translation mechanics when combining foreign subsidiaries</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section 1520</span><span>Income Statement — confirms the absence of any OCI mechanism this Section's self-sustaining treatment must work around</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s17"><span class="num">17</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2">
<div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Integrated foreign operation</div><div class="glossary-def">A foreign operation financially and operationally dependent on the reporting enterprise, translated using the temporal method with results flowing through net income.</div></div>
<div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Self-sustaining foreign operation</div><div class="glossary-def">A financially and operationally independent foreign operation, translated using the current rate method, with the resulting adjustment recognized within a separate equity component rather than net income.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="legend"><div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (ASPE terminology)</div><div class="lrow"><div class="litem"><span class="badge bs">BS</span> Balance Sheet </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge is">IS</span> Income Statement </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge sre">SRE</span> Statement of Retained Earnings / Statement of Changes in Equity </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge scf">SCF</span> Cash Flow Statement</div></div></div>


</div>
  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.aspe1651 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
